WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.834
-Last time you were here,

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:03.235
it was very interesting,
I think, for me.

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:06.339
We were all trying to figure out
how to pronounce your name.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:07.473
-Yeah.

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:10.109
-And now here you are --
You're a major contender

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:12.445
for getting
the Democratic nomination.

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:15.214
You just got a big day today.
The polls came out,

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:18.918
and you're number three
in the polls in Iowa.

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:22.655
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:26.392
What do you attribute
your surge in popularity to?

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:30.663
-Well, hard work, a really good
team, and the right message.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:31.798
I think our message

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:34.067
is what Americans
are really looking for.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.368
It's not just how to end
the Trump presidency,

00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:38.104
which is obviously
a huge priority,

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:40.039
because our country's future
depends on it.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:43.142
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:44.610
But it's also about

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.081
what's America going to need the
day after this presidency ends?

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.884
This presidency will come
to an end one way or the other.

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:54.420
And when we get there, when the
sun comes up over this country,

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.423
and we're picking up the pieces
and figuring out what's next,

00:00:57.423 --> 00:01:00.460
I think it's going to require
that we're ready to undertake

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.162
bold solutions, but also that
we can bring people together.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:05.431
And that's been the core
of our campaign.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:06.833
And I think it's resonated.

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:11.971
-I think, the last debate,

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:15.408
I think you scored major points
for a lot of people during that

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:19.612
because, one, with Elizabeth
Warren, you challenged her

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:23.049
and actually debated, as you
would in a debate, with her

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:25.951
about her Medicare for All.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.951 --> 00:01:28.221
-And you were saying,
"How do you pay for all this?"

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.622
And she didn't really
have an answer.

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:33.092
-It's a respectful, honest,
but major difference of opinion.

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:35.862
First of all, when you're
proposing something like this,

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:37.396
it's got to be clear
how it's paid for.

00:01:37.396 --> 00:01:39.966
And the plan
that I'm putting forward,

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.934
we call it "Medicare
for all who want it,"

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:44.803
can be paid for without raising
middle-class taxes.

00:01:44.803 --> 00:01:46.773
And it's got a lot
of other advantages, too,

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:48.607
because it lets people keep
their private plans.

00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:51.377
So the basic idea is
we take a version of Medicare.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.379
We make it available
for anybody who wants it,

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:55.581
but if for some reason
you prefer a different plan,

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:57.283
that's okay. I'm not going
to make you switch.

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.952
And I just think
that's better than saying,

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:02.655
"You've got to do Medicare
whether you want to or not,"

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.891
and make that entire change
for the entire country

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:07.093
in four years or less.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:09.128
So it's just an honest
difference of opinion.

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.063
-And I saw a lot of people
were saying, like,

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:12.932
"Oh, well, wait a second,
Mayor Pete.

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.134
You said that -- You tweeted out

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:17.603
that you were
for Medicare for All..."

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:19.439
-Yeah.
-"...a couple years ago."

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:20.907
So what do you say to that?

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:23.075
-Yeah, for the long run,
that's still my preference.

00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:25.645
I just think we got to have
some humility about this.

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.947
In other words,
to get from here to there,

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.316
it makes more sense
to make it optional.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.119
And then if we're right, that
that's going to be the best plan

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.854
for all Americans, then
all Americans will choose it,

00:02:35.854 --> 00:02:38.558
and it'll become the only plan.
And if we're wrong,

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:40.560
and some Americans would rather
be on something different,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.161
well, then we're going
to be really glad

00:02:42.161 --> 00:02:43.830
we didn't kick them
off the plans they had

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.998
and force them onto ours.
So it's a similar vision

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:48.334
about where I think
the destination's going to be,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.236
but an approach
that is more practical

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.972
and I think more flexible
for Americans

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:55.274
who I trust to make
the right decision

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:56.743
on what's right for them.

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:59.312
-I think another thing
that where you scored --

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.481
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:05.985
Another area where you scored
in the debate

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.953
is when -- with Beto O'Rourke,

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:10.222
and you kind of, you guys
kind of sparred a little bit

00:03:10.222 --> 00:03:11.924
about gun reform.

00:03:11.924 --> 00:03:16.195
And would you like to talk
about your stance on that and --

00:03:16.195 --> 00:03:19.699
-Well, first of all, I think we
all want to get real gun reform.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:22.101
There's been way too much talk
for way too long.

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:25.170
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.672
And I think the focus right now

00:03:26.672 --> 00:03:28.707
is we've got to have
universal background checks.

00:03:28.707 --> 00:03:30.510
By the way,
Republicans agree on this --

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.011
not so much Republican
members of Congress,

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.412
but the Republicans
across America,

00:03:33.412 --> 00:03:35.381
and Washington still hasn't
been able to get it done.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:36.816
We got to go further than that.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:39.618
We got to have red flag laws
that disarm domestic abusers.

00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:42.454
And we've got to ban the sale
of these assault weapons

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:44.824
and the high-capacity magazines
to load them.

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:46.125
That will save lives in America.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.060
And we've got to do it
right away.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.829
-And that's a --
It's a big issue for you.

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.331
If you do become President,
you'll -- you'll --

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:53.866
that'll be one of your focuses?

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.334
-Yeah, I mean,
we can't go on like this.

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:56.402
You know, I was in high school

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:57.669
when the Columbine
shooting happened.

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.373
And since then, we've generated

00:04:00.373 --> 00:04:03.142
a second
school-shooting generation.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:06.112
Shame on us if we allow there
to be a third.

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:09.782
[ Applause ]

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.517
-And you know these guns

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.252
because you've seen
these assault rifles.

00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:14.520
You were in Afghanistan.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.388
-Yeah.

00:04:16.388 --> 00:04:21.159
-What is your take on everything
with Syria and Turkey and --

00:04:21.159 --> 00:04:24.530
-I got to tell you,
it's just a bad day for America

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:27.066
when our country can't be
relied on to keep its word.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:29.335
You know, I really felt
when I was deployed

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.969
that, in addition
to my body armor

00:04:30.969 --> 00:04:33.139
and the weapons I was carrying,

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.774
that one of the things
that protected me

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:36.943
was that the flag on my shoulder

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.846
represented a country
that was seen as credible,

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:42.314
that our allies
and our enemies knew

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:45.217
that when we said we would do
something, we would do it.

00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:46.753
So when you got
these Kurdish fighters,

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:49.389
they put their lives on the line
to be at our side

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:52.191
fighting against ISIS,
and you betray them,

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.293
which is what
this president has done,

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:57.630
not only is it undermining
stability in Syria,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.099
it's undermining
the honor of American soldiers

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:03.569
who feel now that their honor
has been taken away.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.304
And when you take away
a soldier's honor,

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:07.206
you're taking away
the most important thing.

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:08.908
And it's taking away
our country's honor.

00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:11.477
It makes the world
a more dangerous place.

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:14.146
And when I am president,
when I am Commander in Chief,

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:16.082
we will restore
American credibility,

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:17.717
because lives depend on it.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:20.153
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:28.494
-I know you're in New York,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:32.831
and I saw that you were riding
the subway today, which is --

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:34.967
There you are right there.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.703
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:39.772
I don't know if you were here --

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:42.141
I don't know if you were here
to stop by "Saturday Night Live"

00:05:42.141 --> 00:05:43.809
to ask them to stop doing
an impression of you.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:45.845
I don't know.
But I don't if you saw,

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.113
they did an impression of you.

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:48.281
But they're not on this week.

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:49.749
But yeah, you didn't see it?
-I didn't see it.

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:51.717
[ Laughter ]
-You didn't see it at all?

00:05:51.717 --> 00:05:52.818
-No.
-No, you didn't see it.

00:05:52.818 --> 00:05:54.954
-Was it good?
-Well, we have --

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:57.523
Here's a here's a clip
of Colin Jost playing Mayor Pete

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:00.159
on "Saturday Night Live."
Take a look.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:01.627
-Hi.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:04.096
Thank you very much. Thank you.
I went to Harvard,

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:07.133
but they don't teach you
where to put your arms.

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:09.635
Why am I not winning this,
you know?

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:10.736
I'm a veteran.

00:06:10.736 --> 00:06:13.439
I'm under
the legal retirement age.

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:16.275
And when I talk, it makes sense.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.944
Is something wrong with me?

00:06:17.944 --> 00:06:19.678
-No, no, you're a great guy,

00:06:19.678 --> 00:06:22.448
just, like, as a friend,
not for president.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:23.883
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:26.218
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:26.218 --> 00:06:27.920
-So, you like it?

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:29.888
-It's okay.
-It's okay. It's not --

00:06:29.888 --> 00:06:31.324
It wasn't that bad.

00:06:31.324 --> 00:06:34.127
-Yeah, I just want to say I'm
very comfortable in my own body.

00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:36.128
I know exactly what to do
with my arms.

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:37.730
I don't know --
-But now you're over-thinking

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.032
what to do with your arms now?
-Yeah, now, I'm wondering.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:42.268
Are they too far out,
too far in?

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:44.570
-I'll make it easy for you.
I just want to shake your hand.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.672
Thank you for coming
on our show tonight.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:48.141
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:48.141 --> 00:06:49.575
Mayor Pete Buttigieg, everybody!

